This service already has exactly the right choke points: every write goes through `db.write()` in db.js:13-26, and every request already gets `req.user` from the `sessionUser` middleware in auth.js:5-13. Route handlers (accounts.js, orders.js) never touch the pool directly. That means an audit trail doesn't need touching every route — it needs two additions at the existing seams.

**The fork:** log at the application layer (add a call in `write()`, insert an audit row alongside the write) versus log at the database layer (Postgres triggers on each audited table). App-level is simpler to reason about and lets you write exactly what changed. But it's opt-in per call site — any future write that bypasses `db.write()`, a migration, an admin console query, a `psql` session — silently produces no audit record. For a compliance requirement that means "every write, no exceptions," that gap is disqualifying. I'd go DB-level: an `AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE` trigger on each audited table writing to an append-only `audit_log` table. It can't be forgotten because it isn't called, it fires.

**The "who" problem this creates:** triggers see `OLD`/`NEW` rows but have no idea about `req.user`. Fix it by having `write()` set a Postgres session variable inside the same transaction, then have the trigger read it back:

```js
// db.js
async function write(text, params, actorId) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    if (actorId) await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.actor_id', $1, true)", [actorId]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Route calls pass `req.user.id` as the extra arg (e.g. `db.write(sql, params, req.user.id)`). `set_config(..., true)` scopes it to the transaction, so it's gone by the time the connection returns to the pool — no leakage across pooled connections.

Trigger side:

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  table_name text NOT NULL,
  row_id text NOT NULL,
  action text NOT NULL,
  actor_id integer,
  changed_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  old_row jsonb,
  new_row jsonb
);

CREATE FUNCTION audit_trigger() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log(table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, old_row, new_row)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.actor_id', true), '')::integer,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN NULL;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_trigger();
-- repeat for orders and any other table compliance cares about
```

